---
import ResearchLayout from '../../layouts/ResearchLayout.astro';
import ProjectSection from '../../components/ProjectSection.astro';
import TeamMember from '../../components/TeamMember.astro';
import RelatedLink from '../../components/RelatedLink.astro';
---

<ResearchLayout
  title="Growth-Oriented Practice in Psychosis"
  description="A doctoral research project exploring how mental health professionals can recognise and support growth following psychosis. Led by Sarah Bowles at Salomons Institute, Canterbury Christ Church University."
  pathname="/research/growth-oriented-practice-in-psychosis"
>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="border-b border-border">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 md:px-8 pt-16 md:pt-24 pb-20 md:pb-28">
      <p class="font-accent text-2xl md:text-3xl text-poster-teal mb-4">
        A doctoral research project
      </p>
      <h1 class="font-display font-semibold text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl tracking-tight text-ink" style="text-decoration-color: var(--color-poster-teal)">
        Growth-Oriented Practice in Psychosis
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 font-body text-lg md:text-xl text-muted leading-relaxed">
        What helps people grow through and beyond experiences of psychosis?
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 1: Why this matters -->
  <ProjectSection id="why-this-matters" title="Why this matters" accent="teal">
    <p>
      Psychosis is frequently traumatic, and the experience can cause significant distress and
      disruption. A growing body of research, however, shows that people often also experience
      meaningful growth in its aftermath. This growth can take many forms: a renewed sense of
      purpose, deeper relationships, a stronger sense of self, new priorities, or a richer
      spiritual or existential life.
    </p>
    <p>
      Despite this body of evidence, mental health services rarely have clear models for how
      clinicians can recognise and actively support this kind of growth alongside clinical
      recovery. Without such guidance, clinicians may inadvertently invalidate people's
      experiences or work with the unrealistic expectation that everyone who experiences
      psychosis should grow.
    </p>
    <p>
      This research aims to begin to fill that gap.
    </p>
  </ProjectSection>

  <!-- Section 2: What this research asks -->
  <ProjectSection id="what-this-research-asks" title="What this research asks" accent="coral">
    <p>
      The study explores four questions, from the perspective of clinicians who actively try
      to promote growth following psychosis:
    </p>
    <ol class="space-y-4 mt-2 list-none">
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-body font-semibold text-poster-coral shrink-0 w-6">1.</span>
        <span>How do they understand the ways in which growth can occur following psychosis?</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-body font-semibold text-poster-coral shrink-0 w-6">2.</span>
        <span>How does that understanding shape their clinical practice?</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-body font-semibold text-poster-coral shrink-0 w-6">3.</span>
        <span>What are the core elements of growth-oriented practice, and when should they be applied?</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-4">
        <span class="font-body font-semibold text-poster-coral shrink-0 w-6">4.</span>
        <span>What cultural and ethical considerations shape this kind of work?</span>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </ProjectSection>

  <!-- Section 3: A growing evidence base -->
  <ProjectSection id="evidence-base" title="A growing evidence base" accent="sage">
    <p>
      The idea that psychosis can lead to growth is supported by an expanding research
      literature. A 2018 systematic review synthesised 40 studies of positive change after a
      first episode of psychosis, finding consistent reports of growth at individual,
      interpersonal, and spiritual levels (Jordan et al., 2018). These findings are often
      framed through the lens of post-traumatic growth, the theory that struggling with
      adversity can lead to positive psychological change including greater appreciation for
      life, more meaningful relationships, a sense of personal strength, changed priorities,
      and a stronger spiritual or existential life (Tedeschi &amp; Calhoun, 2004).
    </p>
    <p>
      Other theoretical perspectives have also tried to explain growth following psychosis.
      Jackson's Paradigm Shifting Process Model, for instance, proposes that some psychotic
      experiences may contain insights that allow a person to resolve an underlying internal
      conflict, leading to a meaningful shift in how they understand themselves and the world
      (Jackson, 2010).
    </p>
    <p>
      Despite all this work, very little research has asked clinicians themselves how they
      think about growth following psychosis and how they try to support it. This study is one
      of the first to do so.
    </p>
  </ProjectSection>

  <!-- Section 4: A look at the poster -->
  <ProjectSection id="the-poster" title="A look at the poster" accent="sky">
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/images/poster.png"
        alt="Recruitment poster for the study, featuring a hand-drawn silhouette of a head with flowers growing from it. Title: Growth-Oriented Practice in Psychosis. Subtitle: Call for Participants."
        width="800"
        class="rounded-lg w-full max-w-xl mx-auto block"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption class="mt-4 font-body text-sm text-muted text-center">
        The poster used to invite mental health professionals to take part in the study.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </ProjectSection>

  <!-- Section 5: What participation involves -->
  <ProjectSection id="what-participation-involves" title="What participation involves" accent="peach">
    <p>
      The study involves a single online interview of 30 to 60 minutes, held over Microsoft
      Teams at a time convenient for the participant. Sarah asks a range of questions about
      the participant's experience of working with individuals who have experienced growth
      following psychosis.
    </p>
    <p>
      Interviews are recorded and transcribed via Teams. The transcript is checked for
      accuracy against the recording and any identifying information is removed, after which
      the original recording is deleted. Anonymised quotes from interviews may be used in
      published reports. Participants are not identified in any publication.
    </p>
    <p>
      Participants are free to withdraw their data from the study up to two weeks after the
      interview, without giving any reason.
    </p>
  </ProjectSection>

  <!-- Section 6: Who can take part -->
  <ProjectSection id="who-can-take-part" title="Who can take part" accent="pink">
    <p>Participants are mental health professionals with:</p>
    <ul class="space-y-3 mt-2 list-none">
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="text-poster-teal mt-1.5 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle cx="7" cy="7" r="3"/></svg>
        </span>
        <span>At least one year of experience working with people who have experienced psychosis</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="text-poster-teal mt-1.5 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle cx="7" cy="7" r="3"/></svg>
        </span>
        <span>An awareness of the possibility of growth following psychosis</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-3">
        <span class="text-poster-teal mt-1.5 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle cx="7" cy="7" r="3"/></svg>
        </span>
        <span>An active intention to support clients not only to recover from psychosis but also to learn and grow from it</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </ProjectSection>

  <!-- Section 7: Ethical considerations -->
  <ProjectSection id="ethical-considerations" title="Ethical considerations" accent="teal">
    <p>
      The study has been reviewed and given a favourable opinion by the Salomons Ethics Panel
      at the Salomons Institute for Applied Psychology, Canterbury Christ Church University
      (ethics reference ETH2425-0036).
    </p>
    <p>
      All information collected is kept strictly confidential. Data is encrypted and stored on
      password-protected systems on the Canterbury Christ Church University IT network. Only
      the primary researcher and her supervisors have access. After the project is complete,
      data is retained in encrypted form for ten years in line with university policy, then
      destroyed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Concerns about any aspect of the study can be raised with Sarah directly at{' '}
      <a href="mailto:sb2144@canterbury.ac.uk" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent-hover focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">sb2144@canterbury.ac.uk</a>.
      Concerns that remain unresolved can be raised with her primary supervisor, Dr Anne Cooke
      (<a href="mailto:anne.cooke@canterbury.ac.uk" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent-hover focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">anne.cooke@canterbury.ac.uk</a>).
      Formal complaints can be addressed to Dr Fergal Jones, Clinical Psychology Programme Research Director
      (<a href="mailto:fergal.jones@canterbury.ac.uk" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent-hover focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">fergal.jones@canterbury.ac.uk</a>).
    </p>
  </ProjectSection>

  <!-- Section 8: The research team -->
  <ProjectSection id="the-research-team" title="The research team" accent="coral">
    <p class="text-muted text-sm italic mb-2">These short biographies are published with Anne's and Gerald's approval.</p>
    <div class="space-y-6 mt-4">
      <TeamMember name="Sarah Bowles" role="Lead researcher">
        <p>
          Sarah Bowles is a Trainee Clinical Psychologist in the final year of the Doctoral
          Programme in Clinical Psychology at the Salomons Institute for Applied Psychology,
          Canterbury Christ Church University. Her doctoral research focuses on how mental health
          professionals can recognise and support growth following psychosis. Before training in
          clinical psychology, Sarah worked as a counsellor, a teacher, and an assistant
          psychologist in a range of NHS and third-sector settings in London and Hong Kong. Her
          full background is on the <a href="/about" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent-hover focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">about page</a>.
        </p>
      </TeamMember>

      <TeamMember name="Dr Anne Cooke" role="Primary supervisor">
        <p>
          Dr Anne Cooke is Principal Lecturer and Clinical Director of the Doctoral Programme in
          Clinical Psychology at the Salomons Institute for Applied Psychology, Canterbury Christ
          Church University. She trained at the Institute of Psychiatry in London, qualifying as a
          clinical psychologist in 1992, and worked for many years in NHS mental health services
          in inner-city London before joining Salomons in 1999.
        </p>
        <p>
          Anne's clinical, research, and teaching interests centre on psychological approaches to
          psychosis, community and critical psychology, and promoting public debate about
          taken-for-granted ideas in mental health. She edited the British Psychological Society's
          landmark public information report Understanding Psychosis and Schizophrenia, a
          consensus document developed by 24 contributors drawn from eight universities and six
          NHS trusts together with people who have themselves experienced psychosis. The report
          has had significant national and international influence on how mental health services
          understand and respond to psychosis.
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2017 Anne was named British Psychological Society Practitioner of the Year,
          recognising in particular her work to make high-quality public information about mental
          health widely accessible. She writes regularly for the Salomons blog Discursive of
          Tunbridge Wells.
        </p>
      </TeamMember>

      <TeamMember name="Dr Gerald Jordan" role="Co-supervisor">
        <p>
          Dr Gerald Jordan is Lecturer and Assistant Professor in the School of Psychological
          Science at the University of Bristol, where he leads the Reach Collective, a research
          group investigating post-traumatic growth, peer support, recovery, and citizenship among
          people facing mental health challenges. He received his PhD from McGill University and
          completed postdoctoral fellowships at Yale University and McGill University.
        </p>
        <p>
          Gerald's research examines the positive psychological changes, often described as
          post-traumatic growth, that can follow a first episode of psychosis. His work explores
          what these changes look like, what factors support them, and how mental health services
          can play a meaningful role in facilitating growth alongside clinical recovery. He has
          authored over 50 peer-reviewed publications and has secured research funding from bodies
          in Canada, the UK, and the United States.
        </p>
        <p>
          His writing for general audiences includes essays for Psyche exploring how psychosis,
          while often a profoundly difficult experience, can also inspire growth in areas such as
          a renewed sense of purpose and deeper relationships.
        </p>
      </TeamMember>
    </div>
  </ProjectSection>

  <!-- Section 9: Related work -->
  <ProjectSection id="related-work" title="Related work" accent="sage">
    <p class="text-muted">
      This research is part of a wider conversation about positive change following
      psychosis. The work below offers a starting point for readers who want to explore the
      field further.
    </p>
    <div class="space-y-4 mt-4">
      <RelatedLink
        title="The Reach Collective"
        subtitle="University of Bristol"
        description="Dr Gerald Jordan's research group, examining post-traumatic growth following psychosis and related areas of recovery and citizenship."
        href="https://reach-collective.org"
      />
      <RelatedLink
        title="Understanding Psychosis and Schizophrenia"
        subtitle="British Psychological Society"
        description="The landmark BPS public information report on psychosis, edited by Dr Anne Cooke. Written for service users, families, journalists, policymakers, and mental health workers."
        href="https://www.bps.org.uk/guideline/understanding-psychosis-and-schizophrenia"
      />
      <RelatedLink
        title="EPOCH"
        subtitle="University of Nottingham"
        description="The Experiences of Personal Outcomes from Hearing about recovery study, part of the Research into Recovery group's work on narrative-based approaches to recovery."
        href="https://www.researchintorecovery.com/research/epoch/"
      />
      <RelatedLink
        title="Discursive of Tunbridge Wells"
        subtitle="Salomons blog"
        description="The Salomons blog, with regular writing on critical and psychological approaches to mental health, edited by Anne Cooke and colleagues."
        href="https://blogs.canterbury.ac.uk/discursive/"
      />
    </div>
  </ProjectSection>

  <!-- Section 10: Get in touch -->
  <section id="get-in-touch" aria-labelledby="get-in-touch-heading" class="py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 md:px-8">
      <div class="bg-poster-peach/15 rounded-xl px-8 py-12 text-center space-y-6">
        <h2 id="get-in-touch-heading" class="font-display font-medium text-2xl md:text-3xl text-ink tracking-tight">
          Get in touch
        </h2>
        <p class="font-body text-base md:text-lg text-muted leading-relaxed max-w-prose mx-auto">
          If this work resonates with you and you'd like to discuss it or contribute, please get
          in touch.
        </p>
        <a
          href="mailto:sb2144@canterbury.ac.uk"
          class="inline-block font-display font-semibold text-xl md:text-2xl text-accent hover:text-accent-hover transition-colors underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent"
        >
          sb2144@canterbury.ac.uk
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</ResearchLayout>
